Bound every length-driven XDR decoder against the bytes actually received.

A shared helper, xdr_read_cursor_remaining(), returns the bytes still present
from the read cursor to the end of the iovec chain in 64-bit arithmetic, so no
32-bit wire length can wrap the comparison.  Each length-driven vector and
contig decoder now rejects a declared length (plus its XDR pad) that exceeds
that remaining count before it reads, aliases or copies anything.  The contig
decoders that already open-coded the 64-bit check now express it through the
helper; the vector decoders that lacked the check gain it.

The guard call sites go through xdr_read_cursor_has_remaining(), an inline
wrapper that answers from the current iovec alone when the field fits within it
and only falls back to the full xdr_read_cursor_remaining() sum when the field
spills past it, so the bound stays O(1) for a fixed-size primitive in a
multi-iovec decode.

(git apply ignores this free-text preamble and starts at the diff --git line.)

